want to go to that malarial diagnostics.

00:18:17.600 --> 00:18:26.510
I will hit save, and allow it to append that
gene record.

00:18:26.510 --> 00:18:31.130
Directly to the existing collection.

00:18:31.130 --> 00:18:38.400
New items were added to your collection, so
I will come back to the My NCBI dashboard,

00:18:38.400 --> 00:18:46.050
here is the malarial diagnostics that originally
had three, I am reloading the page, and now

00:18:46.050 --> 00:18:47.540
we have four.

00:18:47.540 --> 00:18:52.550
If I want to make sure the student has access
to this particular collection, right now it

00:18:52.550 --> 00:19:00.830
is private, if I click the private link I
can actually change that to public, from private

00:19:00.830 --> 00:19:09.820
to public, and when I do that and save it,
it will allow me to have an actual URL that

00:19:09.820 --> 00:19:12.290
I can then share with the students.

00:19:12.290 --> 00:19:15.630
And here is the URL, right here.

00:19:15.630 --> 00:19:21.610
That is the URL that I can share with the
student, and this is what the student will

00:19:21.610 --> 00:19:27.450
see, My NCBI Collections, here are the four
records, here are the publications, and here

00:19:27.450 --> 00:19:29.560
is that gene.

00:19:29.560 --> 00:19:34.590
Again there are YouTube videos, and step-by-step
instructions as to how to do exactly what

00:19:34.590 --> 00:19:40.210
I've just shown you so if you have questions
there are plenty of ways depending on whether

00:19:40.210 --> 00:19:44.900
you like videos or step-by-step instructions.

00:19:44.900 --> 00:19:48.820
Another common thing that you often need to
do as a professor is to maintain a bibliography

00:19:48.820 --> 00:19:54.460
listing, a publication listing for various
purposes whether it is populating the publications

00:19:54.460 --> 00:20:00.090
selection of your CV, for the department or
school admin annual report you have to file,

00:20:00.090 --> 00:20:06.500
or to list your productivity under your University
faculty page, your NCBI dashboard has a widget

00:20:06.500 --> 00:20:08.560
called My Bibliography.

00:20:08.560 --> 00:20:15.660
My bibliography is just a specialty collection,
you can see it listed in the collections,

00:20:15.660 --> 00:20:25.090
this collection is for PubMed records, but
you can also add non-NCBI records to it, such

00:20:25.090 --> 00:20:30.520
as non-PubMed journals if you are a chemistry
professor and you have a publication in a

00:20:30.520 --> 00:20:35.870
chemistry journal that is not listed in PubMed,
you can actually manually type it in, textbook

00:20:35.870 --> 00:20:40.450
chapters, book chapters that you have actually
written you can include there as well.

00:20:40.450 --> 00:20:44.060
You can add those to that my bibliography
collection.

00:20:44.060 --> 00:20:48.670
Just as for other collections once you are
happy with it you can make it public and use

00:20:48.670 --> 00:20:52.540
the URL to share or even post on your faculty
page.

00:20:52.540 --> 00:20:54.210
How do you do this?

00:20:54.210 --> 00:20:59.040
Click Manage My bibliography and you can see
the page that looks like this, at the top

00:20:59.040 --> 00:21:04.220
is a link right here where you can make your
bibliography, it is currently private, you

00:21:04.220 --> 00:21:10.580
can make it public, and again use that URL
just like I showed you before.

00:21:10.580 --> 00:21:14.130
And here is the public listing of your publication.

00:21:14.130 --> 00:21:19.710
Then you can add that directly to your faculty
profile page, or your webpage and people will

00:21:19.710 --> 00:21:23.490
automatically see the up to date list.

00:21:23.490 --> 00:21:28.320
If you do it this way you only need to maintain
your my bibliography listing add new publications

00:21:28.320 --> 00:21:33.910
as they come out and anyone who has this link
will always have the up-to-date list.

00:21:33.910 --> 00:21:36.760
Without you having to do any extra work.

00:21:36.760 --> 00:21:41.840
Additionally you can use my bibliography listing
to export the entire list to a citation manager

00:21:41.840 --> 00:21:45.230
for adding references for your next publication.

00:21:45.230 --> 00:21:49.930
You can also use this listing to make a Bio
Sketch to add to your federal grant proposal

00:21:49.930 --> 00:21:53.440
without having to do too much extra work.

00:21:53.440 --> 00:21:59.100
A lot of the instructions about my bibliography
are available in the helpbook, and also the

00:21:59.100 --> 00:22:02.460
step-by-step instructions to share your bibliography.

00:22:02.460 --> 00:22:03.460
www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK53595/

00:22:03.460 --> 00:22:08.670
Now preparing a federal grant proposal is
hard enough, often it gets pretty devious,

00:22:08.670 --> 00:22:13.211
NCBI has worked as a team with a number of
other organizations and federal agencies used

00:22:13.211 --> 00:22:17.460
to create a tool to help you simplify the
Bio Sketch generation process.

00:22:17.460 --> 00:22:21.170
The dashboard widget is called SciENcv.

00:22:21.170 --> 00:22:28.790
It is designed to repurpose the information
that you've already listed in your bibliography

00:22:28.790 --> 00:22:33.430
along with stored information that you put
into the NCBI account and for NIH grants,

00:22:33.430 --> 00:22:35.610
your ERA Commons account.

00:22:35.610 --> 00:22:40.901
It takes this information and populates and
automatically formats your NIH or NSF Bio

00:22:40.901 --> 00:22:44.220
Sketch.

00:22:44.220 --> 00:22:51.280
So again, My Bibliography is a normal collection,
and SciENcv uses that information.

00:22:51.280 --> 00:22:57.720
You can manage SciENcv by creating a new Bio
Sketch, and you can select do I want an NIH

00:22:57.720 --> 00:23:02.370
Bio Sketch or an NSF Bio Sketch and there
are other agencies and organizations coming

00:23:02.370 --> 00:23:04.770
online in the future.

00:23:04.770 --> 00:23:09.230
You can choose your data source whether it
is an existing Bio Sketch or another additional

00:23:09.230 --> 00:23:16.110
things such as ERA Commons or ORCID, then
you can create your Bio Sketch, you can export

00:23:16.110 --> 00:23:18.900
it as a Word document or a PDF.

00:23:18.900 --> 00:23:23.080
Here is an example of an NIH and NSF Bio Sketch.

00:23:23.080 --> 00:23:28.830
Once you have the grant, congratulations,
you're my bibliography will help you monitor

00:23:28.830 --> 00:23:34.250
your compliance with the required NIH Public
access policy and will also assist with the

00:23:34.250 --> 00:23:38.620
preparation of your annual and final reports
because you have your publication listing

00:23:38.620 --> 00:23:40.130
at NCBI.

00:23:40.130 --> 00:23:43.750
We won't get into that right now, in this
webinar, we've produced others that talked

00:23:43.750 --> 00:23:49.660
extensively about this and there are many
document that will help you with this, and

00:23:49.660 --> 00:23:54.230
the handout that you were given, and there
will also be in the blog, will also have links

00:23:54.230 --> 00:24:00.460
to managing NIH public access compliance.

00:24:00.460 --> 00:24:06.020
I want to warn you, or perhaps alert you to
a couple of new developments.

00:24:06.020 --> 00:24:11.640
NCBI has slowly recently been shifting over
to a new design principle, user centered design.

00:24:11.640 --> 00:24:17.110
This means we will be updating our resources
and webpages with more modern and usable layouts,

00:24:17.110 --> 00:24:23.190
based both on what users need to do, and also
rounds of testing with real people even before

00:24:23.190 --> 00:24:25.050
we release them.

00:24:25.050 --> 00:24:29.500
This may impact you in the near future into
specific ways.

00:24:29.500 --> 00:24:35.620
Coming in the fall of 2018, there will be
a new look for the my bibliography widget.

00:24:35.620 --> 00:24:41.350
This is how it currently looks, and here is
a preliminary design of what it might look

00:24:41.350 --> 00:24:43.510
like when is release this fall.

00:24:43.510 --> 00:24:47.840
We have done this to fix issues and solve
concerns that many customers have expressed

00:24:47.840 --> 00:24:51.720
to us, but also come up with a few of our
own ideas about how we could really make things

00:24:51.720 --> 00:24:53.410
better and easier to do.

00:24:53.410 --> 00:24:59.200
We are currently in rounds of testing, but
one thing I would like to make sure you know

00:24:59.200 --> 00:25:05.250
is that everything that you do now, will already
be included in the new system and be fully

00:25:05.250 --> 00:25:08.900
available in the system, you don't need to
wait until it comes out.

00:25:08.900 --> 00:25:13.990
Start getting used to the system, put your
information in and will automatically be converted

00:25:13.990 --> 00:25:14.990
over.

00:25:14.990 --> 00:25:19.580
We hope the new pages will be a whole lot
easier than the current ones.

00:25:19.580 --> 00:25:23.440
As it gets closer to the release of the new
system this fall we will do another webinar

00:25:23.440 --> 00:25:28.060
to show you exactly the what it looks like,
and how it will work.

00:25:28.060 --> 00:25:35.380
And other announcement is that coming in spring
of 2019, there will be a new look for PubMed.

00:25:35.380 --> 00:25:40.580
It's been a while since it has been redesigned,
this is what it looks like now, as I'm sure

00:25:40.580 --> 00:25:45.490
you know, in this recent version, as mobile
devices have become more prevalent there's

00:25:45.490 --> 00:25:49.260
been an attempt to use as a mobile feature
which works fairly well but it doesn't have

00:25:49.260 --> 00:25:53.420
all of the features, including a lot of the
MyNCBI features.

00:25:53.420 --> 00:25:57.880
We would like to make this user interface
more modern, easier to use and better on all

00:25:57.880 --> 00:25:59.720
screens regardless of the size.

00:25:59.720 --> 00:26:07.000
For the past several months we have been running
an experiment called PubMed labs, if you take

00:26:07.000 --> 00:26:11.360
a look at the PubMed labs you may see what
the new version of it might be like, we will

00:26:11.360 --> 00:26:16.280
show you that in a second, please know that
we will have a lot of announcements and webinars

00:26:16.280 --> 00:26:19.920
not only letting you know when this is going
to happen, but also showing you how to use

00:26:19.920 --> 00:26:21.130
the new pages.

00:26:21.130 --> 00:26:27.620
To get a clue as to the direction that we
are going, you can try out PubMed labs, the

00:26:27.620 --> 00:26:30.320
URL is at the top of the page,
www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/labs/pubed

00:26:30.320 --> 00:26:36.180
this is an online fully functional sandbox
for testing out layouts, features and functions,

00:26:36.180 --> 00:26:40.360
with the full set of PubMed data, you won't
actually miss any of the data.

00:26:40.360 --> 00:26:42.290
It is all included in PubMed labs.

00:26:42.290 --> 00:26:48.020
People have expressed to us already that it
seems to be easier to use, and easier to read,

00:26:48.020 --> 00:26:52.280
we've been able to add new functionality that
many people have been asking for.

00:26:52.280 --> 00:26:56.830
Is pretty nice for large screens and computer
desktops but it also seems to work fairly

00:26:56.830 --> 00:27:00.310
well on mobile devices.

00:27:00.310 --> 00:27:04.990
Please keep in mind that PubMed labs is not
yet fully compatible with the NCBI account

00:27:04.990 --> 00:27:11.650
and dashboard, but before it is actually released
at PubMed, and released to the wild it definitely

00:27:11.650 --> 00:27:14.810
will include all of the My Ncbi features.

00:27:14.810 --> 00:27:21.520
I've gone over a lot of stuff, there's always
more things happening at NCBI, if you want

00:27:21.520 --> 00:27:25.970
to keep up with what is happening including
information about new developments, coming

00:27:25.970 --> 00:27:30.960
webinars, when we put out cool new YouTube
tutorials you can scroll to the bottom of

00:27:30.960 --> 00:27:34.790
any of your webpages and click on any of the
social media links.

00:27:34.790 --> 00:27:39.170
Or you can watch a webinar that we did on
keeping up with the NCBI that is on YouTube

00:27:39.170 --> 00:27:40.710
right now.

00:27:40.710 --> 00:27:45.470
Finally if you have any questions or need
help with resources, please do not hesitate

00:27:45.470 --> 00:27:46.740
to send an email to info@ncbi.,nlm.nih.gov.

00:27:46.740 --> 00:27:51.740
And we will get right back to you.

00:27:51.740 --> 00:27:55.830
Thank you very much, and Sherry and I will
be very happy to attempt to answer any questions

00:27:55.830 --> 00:27:56.830
that you might have.

00:27:56.830 --> 00:28:02.220
[Peter Cooper] Thank you Rana, we are at time,
but we will keep it open and answer some of

00:28:02.220 --> 00:28:03.220
these questions.

00:28:03.220 --> 00:28:07.890
The first one I will answer, I think I understand
the answer, and then I will have Sherry and

00:28:07.890 --> 00:28:09.920
Rana talk about some of the other ones.

00:28:09.920 --> 00:28:14.640
One question was how public is the public
URL created for the collection.

00:28:14.640 --> 00:28:19.830
The answer is that URL whoever wants to see
it will have to have the URL, it is not indexed

00:28:19.830 --> 00:28:22.570
by a search engine so no one should be able
to find it.

00:28:22.570 --> 00:28:27.230
The second question is one that we can talk
a little bit about the pros and cons of these

00:28:27.230 --> 00:28:32.600
two approaches, the question is do you recommend
the save search feature or collection feature

00:28:32.600 --> 00:28:36.060
for sharing a systematic review list of resources?

00:28:36.060 --> 00:28:38.650
I will let Sherry address that.

00:28:38.650 --> 00:28:43.970
[Sherry Bailey] Great question, the answer
to that question is it depends on what you

00:28:43.970 --> 00:28:45.120
want to do.

00:28:45.120 --> 00:28:51.850
For the save search, it will give you everything
that is indexed, based on your search.

00:28:51.850 --> 00:28:57.570
So that means something could've been written
for example in 2007, if that is not in your

00:28:57.570 --> 00:29:03.530
search but it was indexed recently, it will
show up in that email saved search.

00:29:03.530 --> 00:29:09.060
If that is all that you care about, are things
related to that topic it is perfect for you.

00:29:09.060 --> 00:29:14.650
For a collection, these are things you want
to save, and refer back to later.

00:29:14.650 --> 00:29:20.530
So honestly the best answer for this question
is you can use them in tandem with each other.

00:29:20.530 --> 00:29:28.920
You can use the save search feature to pull
that documentation for you, all those citations,

00:29:28.920 --> 00:29:36.240
then the ones you want to save and perhaps
share you would put to the collection itself.

00:29:36.240 --> 00:29:38.030
[Peter] Thank you Sherry.

00:29:38.030 --> 00:29:43.030
I think Sherry also addressed the second part
of the question which was about the search

00:29:43.030 --> 00:29:47.990
strategy, which would be present in the email
but not in the collection itself.

00:29:47.990 --> 00:29:52.690
Somebody had encountered problems, they said
if you have a mixed collection, for example

00:29:52.690 --> 00:29:57.550
with Gene results and PubMed results, the
patient would not be able to open the collection.

00:29:57.550 --> 00:29:59.440
I do not know that that is true.

00:29:59.440 --> 00:30:01.120
We will let Sherry talk about that.

00:30:01.120 --> 00:30:06.020
[Sherry] They have been making changes to
how collections work.

00:30:06.020 --> 00:30:13.160
For the particular example that Rana used,
she used a gene as well as a PubMed, you can

00:30:13.160 --> 00:30:17.820
try this out in your own account as well and
I just tried it out here, it does work, it

00:30:17.820 --> 00:30:20.330
depends on what you are trying to do with
the collection.

00:30:20.330 --> 00:30:24.540
If you are just going through and sharing
this information it is fine, if you are taking

00:30:24.540 --> 00:30:29.570
the information and trying to incorporate
it into another collection, that is where

00:30:29.570 --> 00:30:33.220
you will run into a little bit of a problem,
we are working on that as well.

00:30:33.220 --> 00:30:39.480
[Peter] Okay, those of the questions that
we have so far, so I will go ahead and ask

00:30:39.480 --> 00:30:44.019
Rana to end the webinar and thank you all
for coming, we will talk to you next time.

